+
+Supply both paths with repeated --emoji-spanish-annotations for a full rebuild;
+the builder verifies their hashes before import. To reproduce only Spanish
+enrichment from the current English catalog, run from apps/keyboard-ios after
+downloading those files to /tmp/cldr-es.xml and /tmp/cldr-derived-es.xml:
+
+    PYTHONPATH=scripts python3 - <<'PY'
+    import json
+    from pathlib import Path
+    from build_language_data import add_spanish_emoji_names, verify_spanish_emoji_sources
+
+    paths = [Path('/tmp/cldr-es.xml'), Path('/tmp/cldr-derived-es.xml')]
+    verify_spanish_emoji_sources(paths)
+    path = Path('LanguageData/emoji.json')
+    catalog = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
+    add_spanish_emoji_names(catalog, paths)
+    path.write_text(json.dumps(catalog, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(',', ':')), encoding='utf-8')
+    PY
+    python3 -m unittest discover -s scripts -p 'test_emoji*.py'
+
+Only speech names for existing base sequences are joined, ignoring VS16 for
+lookup. Every English field and category/entry order is conserved. There are
+1,914 Spanish names; nine Unicode18 entries have no CLDR48 name. The resource
+adds 44,744 bytes (286,829 total); SHA-256
+d201157dd725d8e8af6ef91ed56aee867bf52dc0d3cb314e649a8614b3876edd.
+
+Spanish prefixes fold vowel accents and preserve consonant marks, including ñ.
+English and Spanish words can be combined conjunctively. Legacy English results
+lead the list. A fixed reservation from actual English postings and exact folded
+names keeps localized membership stable across the canonical identity's word
+order/repetition variants. That identity retains Spanish tokens, legacy-normalized
+words and exact folded-name words separately; arbitrary raw punctuation/mark
+changes need not preserve it. Conservative reservations can leave unused slots.
+The R40 audit defines the exact policy and gates; resource enrichment alone makes
+no usefulness or performance claim.
